Why Apache-2.0

This benchmark is built on four open-vocabulary detectors, and the licence
follows theirs rather than being chosen independently. Every upstream component
below was checked against the licence file or package metadata on the machine
the results were produced on, not from memory:

  Grounding DINO      Apache-2.0   (LICENSE in the upstream checkout)
  mmdetection         Apache-2.0   (package metadata; provides GLIP)
  transformers        Apache-2.0   (package metadata; provides OWLv2, OmDet-Turbo)
  PEFT                Apache-2.0   (package metadata)

GLIP's original release from Microsoft is MIT, which is more permissive than
Apache-2.0 and imposes nothing that conflicts with it; this repository calls
mmdetection's port rather than that code directly. Apache-2.0 is therefore the
most restrictive licence in the stack and the one the combined work can carry.

One exception aside, none of these projects is vendored here: this repository
imports them, so what it distributes is its own code plus the attributions
below. The exception is part of `models/gdino/lora`, carried because the checkout
it used to be imported from has never committed those files -- see that
package's docstring, and the entry under Third-party code.

